# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two FILES, never a
# shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is defined in run_gates.sh.
# Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
register_gate "tests/test_column_impact.py" "COLUMN IMPACT GATE: PASS" "column-impact precision: column-level schema-change (rename/drop COLUMN) pinpoint is precise ONLY when table-qualified; bare/ubiquitous column refs do NOT fan out; measured NO on emitting column EDGES, boundary pinned" "column-impact precision MEASURE-FIRST result (audit 2026-06-19, parallel to schema-precision PR #254): one granularity BELOW the existing TABLE-level schema graph -- a migration that RENAMES/DROPS a COLUMN (\`voucher.used\`, \`stockrecord.price_excl_tax\`) -> the EXACT code referencing it. MEASURED time-correct (predict at each migration's PARENT commit where the old name still exists; ground truth = the non-migration files the migration's OWN commit edited = the human blast radius) on saleor/netbox/django-oscar: BARE column-name resolution (R0) and R0+ubiquitous-guard (R1) hit only 19-39% precision (61-81% FALSE couplings) at 17-27% recall -- wall-paper noise the CARDINAL RULE forbids; R1==R0 because renamed columns are mostly already SPECIFIC so the ubiquitous guard rarely fires. TABLE-QUALIFIED resolution (R2: tie the column to its table) is 38-100% precise but recall ~0.4-2% -- because the real reference is almost always a LOWERCASE INSTANCE attr (\`voucher.used\`, not \`Voucher.used\`) and tying instance->model->table needs TYPE INFERENCE a content-free static regex cannot do. HONEST DECISION = a measured NO: DO NOT add column-level coupling EDGES (R0/R1 floods 60-80% false; R2 adds ~nothing over the EXISTING table-level schema coupling, which already surfaces the migration<->code link via the shared table name, corroborated by file-level co-change). Extractor changed NOTHING; boundary PINNED here. This gate (hermetic synthetic, content-free: table+column NAMES + paths only, no DB/network) proves: (1) the migration parser extracts (table,column,change_type) from Django RenameField/RemoveField/AddField + raw DDL ALTER RENAME/DROP COLUMN + Rails rename_column; (2) the QUALIFIED resolver PINPOINTS \`Users.user_id\` for a users.user_id rename; (3) PRECISION -- an unrelated \`Order.user_id\` of a DIFFERENT table and a BARE ubiquitous \`.id\` are NOT coupled; (4) the emission rule is table-qualified ONLY and an untieable ref is DROPPED (don't-emit-when-ambiguous = recall-poor by design). A future 'column impact' feature that fans out bare/ubiquitous column names FAILS here." "column-impact precision: column-level schema-change pinpoint is precise only when table-qualified (bare/ubiquitous column refs over-couple 60-80% false, measured on saleor/netbox/django-oscar); table-qualify is precise but recall ~0 (instance attrs need type inference); measured NO on column EDGES, boundary pinned against a future bare-column-fan-out" 16
